iam actually begins the book in the  preface or the the the introduction  actually by saying look you know this is  a bit difficult in terms of the writing  but you're just gonna have to grow into  it understandably so he says I'm not  going to try to explain it as to the  average twelve-year-old because you're  not an average twelve-year-old so you  may want to leave the book on the shelf  for a few more years I think it's  perfectly fair point what is the book  really about so I mean the title is  meditations on self-discipline and  failure and I think that it's an  interesting idea that the discussion of  failure it's really an attempt to try to  provide a stoic perspective of sorts on  all sorts of things in contemporary life  to provide advice that might be useful  to somebody so if you're looking for  something that I would say is at least  in most respects authentically stoic and  Jen you know genuinely is the product of  somebody who's read the stomach text  says has assimilated them has thought a  lot about them that I think this is a  good book for you I'd recommend it  what are some of the key ideas of this  book that I think will help you make a  decision whether you want to get  yourself a copy of it or not so I'm  gonna run through these in kind of a  bullet point fashion not dwelling on  them very long but just telling you what  some of the the key themes and doctrines  are so it's very clear that this is a  book for the self-reliant or the person  who wants to be talked into being  self-reliant there's a strong suspicion  of politics as largely theater this  comes up in a number of places I would  say one of the key messages of the book  over and over again is that we should  toughen up face reality don't be weak we  should take responsibility for our vices  for the things that are wrong with us  and work on the virtues I don't want to  give the impression that it that it's  totally uncompromising or that it's it's  a you know sort of writing everybody off  it I think it meets the reader who does  want to work on their their issues one  of the really interesting aspects of a  book is this discussion of well what do  we do with our failures because we're  going to be failing an awful lot I think  this is totally correct about the the  stoic path and about in a moral  philosophy applied to life in general  we're always gonna have to figure out  how to pick ourselves up and and move on  from our failures and what can we learn  from them another key idea is that we  were given a lot of things that the  Stoics called externals and indifference  like our financial means right or income  our relationships with other people our  social status all of those things we  ought to use prudently without getting  too attached to them so that we we get  very upset when things don't go our way  with them another thing that I think is  quite interesting so that there is a  strong emphasis on appreciation and  gratitude for what it is that we do have  which i think is a positive mess  of the book don't expect or feel  entitled to anything from anyone it's  taking the sort of epic TD and what is  in our power what's not in our power you  know focus just on what's in your power  to about as much of an extreme as it can  he he also talks about anger and temper  and forgiveness that comes up quite  quite frequently the need to restrain  our anger to you know realize why we  lost our temper to forgive others not to  hold grudges and then I think another  really interesting aspect of the book is  he talks about getting small and this  ties in with that issue of externals  it's sort of the opposite of the body  builders get big the body builder is  focused primarily just on the body right  which is an external from the stoic  perspective william clearly is somebody  who is interested in exercise and a  Cesis but the idea is to get ourselves  small to pare ourselves down to the  minimum and you know to to that in that  way the Stoics thought we could free  ourselves we could keep ourselves from  being as they called it hindered and we  could concentrate on what's really  valuable in life the last theme that I  want to hit on again very typically  Orthodox stoic idea life is short  things are passing away we should put  things in the proper perspective so that  we can in fact act appropriately with  the the things that we do have  I'd like to say a little bit about what  I particularly liked about the book and  what I think are some real positive  aspects to it these might not be things  that appeal to everybody but but you  know some of this has to do with our own  personal characters and temperaments so  this is this is definitely not going to  be a book that everybody is going to  like to read I enjoyed it quite a bit  but I also enjoy reading Epictetus and  the stories about the cynics so this  this kind of diatribe genre fits in well  for me I also really really liked  Williams emphasis on a lot of  contemporary issues he's he's definitely  thinking out how stoicism can apply to  our own days and times he talks a lot  about the media about contemporary  politics about things like terrorism and  crime the cult of celebrity that we have  about the abortion itself about you know  the need for meditation there's a good  sort of Irenaeus kind of a kind of  approach to matters of religion and and  God he you know he takes the stance of  well we don't really know and and even  if God doesn't exist we can still have a  you know a good moral life within this  universe a valuable life I mentioned  that that I think that there's some good  discussion here about anger and  forgiveness and also the attitude of  gratitude and I see those as in many  respects sort of typical Ciceronian and  and in some respects Sennett Seneca type  of stuff so it's not all straight out  Epictetus I think that that this is a  fairly faithful representation of  certain aspects of what stoicism has to  offer  on the whole I think this is a quite a  good book there are a few aspects of it  that I myself find problematic I think  that they're more matters of emphasis  than you know total disagreements with  anything I think that this is a good  example of a type of stoicism that maybe  veers more towards classical cynicism in  certain respects particularly in its  disengagement from the the political and  cultural spheres it's almost dismissal  of those there's also a discussion in  there about tribalism in Chapter 8 that  I myself I find a bit out of sorts with  the stoic notion of cosmopolitanism this  is the sort of thing where I think you  could you can see something like this in  for example Cicero but even Cicero  thinks that we have to you know think in  terms of the universal human race  there's also a discussion in in Chapter  9 about happiness that almost seems  rather conch in rather than stoic saying  that that happiness really isn't our  goal that that living right is is our  goal and I mean you know for the classic  Stoics and I think for contemporary  Stoics of most sorts eudaimonia living  well that's that's really happiness  that's really what the goal is but you  can't live well without living rightly  so I don't I don't think that we want to  split those apart quite so much and  there is a you know somewhat troubling  emphasis on we have to resist violence  with violence  Williams seems to say at certain points  for example in chapter 10 in chapter 24  that once somebody crosses a certain  line no no holds barred for them and  this is something I need to think about  a bit more you know how do we how do we  pose a modern nihilistic terrorism or or  you know certain kinds of criminal  activity  or you know unjust you know military  aggression how do we resist these sorts  of things as Stoics I think there's  there's a lot of discussion that still  needs to go on it can't just be well  once the line is crossed anything goes  but those are like I said I think more  matters of 